                    {% else %}<span class="badge badge-unknown">UNKNOWN</span>{% endif %}
                </td>
                <td>{{ rk.details }}</td>
                <td><span class="pill pill-mitre">{{ rk.mitre }}</span></td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </table>

        <div class="callout callout-danger no-break">
            <div class="callout-title">&#9760; Technical Analysis of Direct Kernel Object Manipulation (DKOM)</div>
            The process <strong>{{ data.hidden_processes[0].name if data.hidden_processes else 'rootkit_stealth.exe' }}</strong> (PID: {{ data.hidden_processes[0].pid if data.hidden_processes else 6666 }}) exhibits classic DKOM behavior. The adversary directly altered the <code>Flink</code> (forward link) and <code>Blink</code> (backward link) pointers of the <code>EPROCESS.ActiveProcessLinks</code> structure. Standard Windows kernel APIs (including <code>EnumProcesses</code>, <code>CreateToolhelp32Snapshot</code>, and <code>NtQuerySystemInformation</code>) rely solely upon traversing this circular linked list; consequently, the malicious process became completely invisible to user-mode monitors and Task Manager.
        </div>

        <div class="callout callout-info no-break">
            <div class="callout-title">&#128737; Cross-View Forensic Proof of Execution</div>
            While the process was unlinked from the high-level process list, Compy V2's deep memory pool scanner verified its physical existence by locating its uncorrupted <code>_EPROCESS</code> structure in Non-Paged Pool memory tagged with the pool identifier <code>Proc</code>. Furthermore, verification of the thread dispatcher confirmed <strong>8 scheduled execution threads</strong> running actively on the CPU at the instant of RAM acquisition. This cross-view discrepancy constitutes irrefutable forensic proof of stealth rootkit execution.
        </div>
    </div>

